<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cheshire is a county in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the Welsh border and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> as its historic county town. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it formed part of the territory of the Cornovii, and its brine springs have been exploited for salt since prehistoric times. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> it became the County Palatine of Chester, whose earls held quasi-royal powers, and it kept separate palatine institutions until 1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheshire.jpg" alt="Cheshire" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_view_north_west_from_Beeston_Castle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4547395_(edited,_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cheshire covers 905 square miles (2,344 km2), between Merseyside, Greater Manchester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a> and the Welsh counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flintshire">Flintshire</a> and Wrexham, with a short coastline on the Dee Estuary. The Cheshire Plain, flat land of glacial till left by the retreat of a Quaternary ice sheet, is dotted with meres, the water-filled kettle holes. To the east the land rises to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> and part of the Peak District. The bedrock is almost entirely red Triassic sandstone, quarried at Runcorn and used for Liverpool Cathedral, Chester Cathedral and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beeston-castle">Beeston Castle</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name was first recorded in 980 as Legeceasterscir in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, 'the shire of the city of legions', after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>; the county is thought to have been created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> around 920. After 1066 local resistance was put down in 1069 as part of the Harrying of the North, and the lands of landowners such as Earl Edwin of Mercia were given to Norman barons. William I made Cheshire a county palatine. Hugh d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a>, nicknamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-davranches-earl-of-chester">Hugh Lupus</a>, became Earl of Chester about 1070, with autonomous powers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welsh-marches">Welsh Marches</a>. Magna Carta did not apply to the shire, so the earl issued his own Chester Charter.</p><p>In 1397 Richard II added lands in the Welsh march and raised Cheshire to a principality, in reward for the support of the Cheshire Guard, his standing force of about 500 men. It lost the rank on his fall in 1399.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cheshire is a ceremonial county of North West England with a population of 1,139,884 in 2024; its largest settlement is Warrington and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> lies in the west. Four unitary authorities carry out local government: Cheshire East, Cheshire West and Chester, Halton and Warrington. The county council was abolished on 1 April 2009. The county is known for Cheshire cheese, salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and its farming is based mainly on dairying. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill forts and the Roman fortress and walls of Chester survive, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewis-carroll">Lewis Carroll</a> named the Cheshire Cat after the county.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cheshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early kingdom probably stretched from the Welsh uplands across the upper Severn valley and into the plains of what became Shropshire and <b>Cheshire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman authors accuse the Gauls of human sacrifice; some finds, such as Lindow Man, killed and laid in a <b>Cheshire</b> bog probably in the 1st century CE and found in 1984, may support them, but how common the practice was is debated.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cornovii occupied Shropshire, <b>Cheshire</b> and parts of Staffordshire and the Welsh borderland in the late Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornovii">Cornovii</a></li><li><blockquote>The earls held nearly all of <b>Cheshire</b>, with their own courts, sheriff and barons, and royal writs did not normally run there, a status later called palatine.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-chester">County Palatine of Chester</a></li></ul></section>
